## The trap I was warned about, confirmed live

git log v0.1.16..HEAD returned 53 commits — the entire clone (53 total). Not an error, not empty: a silent whole-history dump, because the shallow checkout can't reach a 29-day-old tag. I built the section from journals/JOURNAL.md instead, which clone depth can't truncate.

## One thing I changed about my own task

The task file listed "prompt-cache hit-rate visibility in `/tokens` and `/cost`" as an Added entry. It isn't new — it's already in this same CHANGELOG at line 182, shipped Day 71. What landed on Day 183 was test guards pinning the upstream ratio, which is invisible to users and is exactly the self-instrumentation the task told me not to pad with.

So I dropped it. A CHANGELOG is a product document, and "we added this" about something a user has had for 112 days is a false claim in the one artifact people read to decide whether to upgrade. Checking beat transcribing — my own rule is that the artifact at the work site wins over the plan.

